  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Karamojong\"\nsubtitle: \"Uganda\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Karamojong, also called Karimojong, are a Nilotic ethnic group. |\n| Where | They live mainly in north-east Uganda, in southern Karamoja. |\n| How many | About 475,000 people. |\n| Language | Ngakarimojong, a Nilotic language in the Nilo-Saharan family. |\n| Religion | Traditional beliefs are reflected in the language; the current religious landscape is not described. |\n| Known for | Livestock herding · Pastoral transhumance · Wrestling linked with marriage · Traditional dance · The social importance of cattle |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Karamojong are agro-pastoral herders whose main livelihood is keeping livestock. Crop growing is secondary and possible only in suitable areas. Their culture includes age-based social organization, seasonal movement with animals, wrestling linked to marriage, and traditional dance. Cattle have strong social and cultural importance.\n\n## Material culture\n\nKaramojong material culture includes hide clothing, beadwork, metal tools, weapons, gourds, wooden vessels, and food-storage containers.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nThe profile records beadwork, hide work, cowrie shells, and fibre decoration, but no broad named textile tradition.\n\n- **Beaded women’s aprons** — These aprons use glass beads arranged in patterns on hide backed with hair.\n- **Cowrie-shell girdles** — Women’s girdles were made with cowrie shells, hide, ostrich eggshell discs, and glass beads.\n- **Married woman’s tail-piece** — This tail is made of vegetable fibre and decorated with pink, white, and blue glass beads.\n- **Hide arm ornaments** — These ornaments use five plaited hide strips, tassels, and sometimes coils of aluminium wire.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nWomen’s dress includes hide front pieces, beaded aprons, girdles, tail-pieces, and open sandals, while headgear is recorded for both men and women.\n\n- **Married woman’s front piece** — This front piece is made of hide and was worn as part of a married woman’s dress.\n- **Unmarried woman’s front piece** — This smaller front piece could be decorated with cowrie shells and beads.\n- **Buffalo-hide sandals** — Open sandals were made from buffalo hide.\n- **Composite feather headdress** — This headdress uses black ostrich feathers mounted on cow hide with a fibre chin strap.\n- **Hair-and-clay chignon** (*Chignon*) — This head-dress is made of human hair and clay and painted brown, blue, and grey.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nEveryday objects include metal tools and weapons, clay pots, gourds, wooden vessels, and containers for storing food.\n\n- **Club** (*rungu*) — Clubs are called rungu.\n- **Finger-knife** — A finger-knife has a leather guard.\n- **Leaf-headed arrows** — These arrows have leaf-shaped iron heads.\n- **Clay pots** — Clay pots without ears were recorded from Kangole.\n- **Food-storage vessels** — Cylindrical wooden or other vessels, called drums in some records, were used to store food.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nBody adornment uses hide, cowrie shells, beads, metal, hair, clay, and stone.\n\n- **White-stone lip ornament** — A man’s lip ornament was made from white stone.\n- **Spiral child’s bracelet** — A child’s bracelet consists of aluminium and copper spirals on a metal core.\n- **Iron neck ornament** — A woman’s neck ornament is made of iron with rolled ends.\n- **Black-bead waist ornament** — This waist ornament consists of five loops made mostly from black glass beads.\n- **Painted hair ornament** — A frontal head ornament was made from felted human hair and clay and then painted.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nThe sources do not name Karamojong musical genres or instruments, though they record a dance bell and objects called drums that were used for food storage.\n\n- **Dance bell** — A girl wore a bell below the knee during dance.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nTraditional Karamojong dance is described as remarkable and enduring, but no particular dance or theatre form is named.\n\n- **Traditional dance** — The sources describe Karamojong traditional dance as remarkable and lasting over time.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nThe profile describes seasonal livestock movement, marriage wrestling, and an offering against illness and accidents, but no annual festival calendar.\n\n- **Pastoral transhumance** — For three to four months each year, people move their animals to nearby districts for water and pasture.\n- **Marriage wrestling** — A young man must wrestle the woman he wants to marry, and winning allows him to marry her and begin dowry talks.\n- **Marriage wrestling for outsiders** — A non-Karamojong man who wants to marry a Karamojong woman must also undergo the wrestling ceremony.\n- **Illness-and-accident offering** — A goat-hide strap was worn within a family during an offering against illness and accidents.\n\n## Foodways\n\nFoodways center on cattle, milk, water, and stored food, while crop growing is secondary.\n\n- **Milk vessel** — A milk vessel from Nabilutuk is recorded in a museum collection.\n- **Calabash bowl** — Calabash bowls were among the recorded household vessels.\n- **Gourd water vessel** — Water vessels were made from gourds and carried with leather straps.\n- **Stored-food vessel** — Several wooden or other vessels were used to store food.\n- **Water** (*ŋakipi*) — The Ngakarimojong word for water is ŋakipi.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nMigration stories explain Karamojong origins and the meaning of their name, while the sources also record greetings and proverbs.\n\n- **Abyssinian migration tradition** — One tradition says the group migrated from Abyssinia between 1600 and 1700 AD before splitting into several peoples.\n- **Ethiopian migration tradition** — Another account places the wider migration from present-day Ethiopia around 1600.\n- **Name-origin phrase** (*ekar ngimojong*) — The name Karamojong is linked to ekar ngimojong, explained as “the old men can walk no farther.”\n- **Greeting** (*Ejoka?*) — Ejoka? is a recorded greeting.\n- **Greeting response** (*Ejok-nooi*) — Ejok-nooi is the recorded response to the greeting.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nNgakarimojong is a written Nilotic language with several tones, related languages, and vocabulary that reflects traditional beliefs.\n\n- **Ngakarimojong** — Ngakarimojong is spoken by the Karamojong in north-east Uganda.\n- **Alternative spelling** (*ŋaKarimojoŋ*) — ŋaKarimojoŋ is one Karamojong spelling of the language name.\n- **God** (*Akuj*) — Akuj is the word for God, and its root also means “north” and “up.”\n- **Roman alphabet** — Written Ngakarimojong uses the Roman alphabet.\n- **Language tones** — The language has three basic tones and three contour tones.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Ngakarimojong* — Karamojong language\n- *ŋaKarimojoŋ* — Karamojong spelling of the language name\n- *rungu* — club\n- *Chignon* — hair-and-clay head-dress\n- *ŋakipi* — water\n- *Akuj* — God\n- *ekar ngimojong* — the old men can walk no farther\n- *Ejoka?* — greeting\n- *Ejok-nooi* — greeting response\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- John Lamphear, *The Traditional History of the Jie of Uganda*, Clarendon Press, 1976.\n- Diane Lesley-Neuman, *ATR Harmony in Karimojong: Justification for a Stratal Optimality Theory*, University of Colorado, 2007.\n- P. Logiro and J. Ilukori, *A Simplified Ngakarimojong-English English-Ngakarimojong Dictionary*, Fountain Publishers, 2007.\n- Bruno Novelli, *A Grammar of the Karimojong Language*, D. Reimer, 1985.\n- [Karamojong people](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karamojong_people)\n- [Karamojong language](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karamojong_language)\n",
